   6,
   6,
   [
    1,
    "hetu",
    "root"
   ],
   "The six roots are root condition to the cittas and factors they arise with and to the matter those cittas produce."
  ],
  [
   "jhāna factors",
   7,
   5,
   [
    17,
    "jhāna",
    "jhāna"
   ],
   "The seven jhāna factors are jhāna condition to the states they arise with and to mind-born matter: they make the mind 'contemplate' its object closely."
  ],
  [
   "path factors",
   12,
   9,
   [
    18,
    "magga",
    "path"
   ],
   "Path factors are path condition: they lead the mind toward a destination, good or bad."
  ],
  [
   "faculties (indriya)",
   22,
   16,
   [
    16,
    "indriya",
    "faculty"
   ],
   "Faculty condition covers 20 of the 22: femininity and masculinity are excluded (Nyanatiloka, Buddhist Dictionary, 'paccaya'). The 5 sensitivities are prenascent faculty condition to the sense consciousnesses; physical life to the kamma-born matter of its kalāpa; the mental faculties to the states they arise with."
  ],
  [
   "powers (bala)",
   9,
   9,
   null,
   "No condition of its own: the powers are the same factors seen as unshakeable by their opposites."
  ],
  [
   "predominants (adhipati)",
   4,
   4,
   [
    3,
    "adhipati",
    "predominance"
   ],
   "The four predominants are the conascent kind of predominance condition: one at a time, in javanas with two or three roots. Object predominance (a strongly esteemed object) is the other kind and is not part of this category."
  ],
  [
   "nutriments (āhāra)",
   4,
   4,
   [
    15,
    "āhāra",
    "nutriment"
